    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 8\n    nc = 7\n    facility_coords = [(49, 55), (94, 30), (64, 38), (79, 39), (73, 21), (85, 73), (63, 51), (82, 2)]\n    customer_coords = [(86, 25), (57, 14), (59, 13), (8, 40), (80, 76), (60, 41), (3, 42)]\n    capacities = [95.0, 125.0, 195.0, 88.0, 117.0, 157.0, 186.0, 114.0]\n    demands = [26.0, 38.0, 50.0, 46.0, 14.0, 41.0, 20.0]\n    fixed_costs = [458.0, 309.0, 310.0, 272.0, 245.0, 121.0, 259.0, 336.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: F3 requires F7\n    m.addConstr(open_f[3] <= open_f[7])\n\n    # Policy: min throughput 7 per open facility\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) >= 7.0*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: opening budget cap $1011\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 1011.0)\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Distribution Network Decision \u2014 Warehouse Activation and Customer Fulfillment Plan\n\nExecutive summary\nWe are preparing a network design recommendation that determines which candidate warehouses to activate and how to route shipments to each customer so that all demand is met at the lowest total landed cost. The total landed cost includes the fixed cost to activate selected facilities and the variable cost of transportation, which is based on distance and shipment volume. The decision must respect capacity limits at each warehouse and the company\u2019s mandatory operating policies outlined below.\n\nDecision objective\n- Choose the subset of candidate warehouses to activate and determine shipment volumes from each activated warehouse to each customer to minimize the sum of:\n  - Facility activation costs\n  - Transportation costs driven by distance and units moved (at the corporate per\u2011kilometer, per\u2011unit rate specified in the Annex)\n- Ensure full, on\u2011time fulfillment of every customer\u2019s demand while staying within each facility\u2019s available capacity.\n\nBusiness requirements\n- Customer service:\n  - Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied; no shortfalls or deferrals are permitted.\n- Facility operations:\n  - A warehouse may only ship product if it has been activated.\n  - Each facility has a finite throughput capacity and cannot ship more than its available capacity.\n  - Every activated facility must handle at least 7 units of total outbound shipments across all customers to justify operating overhead.\n- Cost and budget discipline:\n  - Opening a warehouse incurs a fixed activation cost before any shipments can depart.\n  - Transportation cost is volume\u2011 and distance\u2011based, calculated using the per\u2011unit, per\u2011kilometer rate listed in the Annex.\n  - The capital budget for facility activation is capped at $1011; the sum of fixed activation costs for all warehouses we choose to activate must not exceed this amount.\n- Network structure policy:\n  - The site labeled F3 at coordinates (79, 39) depends on the regional hub F7 at (82, 2). If F3 is activated, F7 must also be activated. F3 cannot operate independently of F7.\n\nScope and approach\n- Scope covers all candidate warehouse locations and all customers provided in the data set, with the intention to balance fixed activation costs against transportation costs while maintaining service and capacity discipline.\n- Distances will be derived from the provided map coordinates using the company\u2019s standard, consistent distance measure.\n- Shipments may be allocated from one or multiple activated warehouses to satisfy each customer\u2019s demand, provided capacity, budget, and policy requirements are met.\n- The recommendation will be supported by scenario testing around the budget cap, minimum\u2011throughput policy, and hub dependency to ensure results are robust and implementable.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended list of warehouses to activate within the $1011 capital budget.\n- Customer\u2011by\u2011warehouse shipment plan that satisfies all demand and honors facility capacity limits and the minimum\u2011throughput requirement of 7 units for each activated site.\n- Cost breakdown showing activation spend and transportation spend at the transport rate listed in the Annex.\n- Sensitivity insights on the trade\u2011off between activating more facilities (higher fixed cost, lower transport cost) versus fewer facilities (lower fixed cost, potentially higher transport cost), and the implications of the F3\u2013F7 dependency.\n\nImplementation considerations\n- The minimum\u2011throughput policy may lead us to consolidate low\u2011volume flows at fewer activated sites to avoid operating below 7 units per site.\n- The F3\u2013F7 dependency implies that F3 will only be considered when the combined economics of opening both F3 and F7 remain within the $1011 budget and improve total landed cost.\n- Capacity headroom will be validated so that the proposed plan can absorb routine variability without breaching facility limits or the minimum\u2011throughput policy.\n\nNext steps\n- Validate the input data set with Operations and Finance.\n- Run the network design analysis under the stated policies and budget cap.\n- Review preliminary results with stakeholders, iterate on any service-area exceptions, and finalize the activation and shipping plan.\n\nAnnex \u2014 Data inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (49, 55)\n  - F1: (94, 30)\n  - F2: (64, 38)\n  - F3: (79, 39)\n  - F4: (73, 21)\n  - F5: (85, 73)\n  - F6: (63, 51)\n  - F7: (82, 2)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (86, 25)\n  - C1: (57, 14)\n  - C2: (59, 13)\n  - C3: (8, 40)\n  - C4: (80, 76)\n  - C5: (60, 41)\n  - C6: (3, 42)\n- Facility capacities: [95.0, 125.0, 195.0, 88.0, 117.0, 157.0, 186.0, 114.0]\n- Customer demands: [26.0, 38.0, 50.0, 46.0, 14.0, 41.0, 20.0]\n- Facility fixed activation costs: [458.0, 309.0, 310.0, 272.0, 245.0, 121.0, 259.0, 336.0]\n- Transport rate: 0.5",
